N('old-mackinac-point-light-mackinaw-city','Old Mackinac Point Light','Mackinaw City','mi','light','standing',45.7875,-84.72946,'exact','open','walk','','1892–1957',
  'A castle-style light station built in 1892 at the tip of the Lower Peninsula, on a limestone foundation, with the tower and keeper\'s house joined and both made of Cream City brick trimmed with Indiana limestone. It was first lit on October 25, 1892. It was switched off when the Mackinac Bridge opened; the articles give 1957 and 1958 for the end, and this entry does not choose between them. It is now a museum, restored to look as it did about 1910.',
  'The tower and keeper\'s quarters with period furnishings on the ground floor, hands-on lighthouse exhibits, and guided tours up the tower to the lantern room. Entry is through the 1907 Fog Signal Building, which is the museum shop. The brick is pale buff, not red.',
  'A castle-shaped lighthouse beside the Mackinac Bridge.',
  'Run by Mackinac State Historic Parks beside Colonial Michilimackinac, in Michilimackinac State Park. Admission is charged (adults $10.75 and children 5 to 12 $7.50 in the 2025 brochure). Hours below are from the park\'s 2024 and 2025 brochures as a web search quoted them; the 2026 season was not read, and the site is shut in winter. Parking is in the lots on North Huron Avenue next to the park.',
  [W('Old Mackinac Point Light'),['Mackinac State Historic Parks: Old Mackinac Point Lighthouse','https://www.mackinacparks.com/parks-and-attractions/old-mackinac-point-lighthouse/'],['Mackinac State Historic Parks: 2025 lighthouse brochure (PDF)','https://www.mackinacparks.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/2025-Old-Point-Lighthouse-Brochure.pdf']],
  conf='mixed',th=[45.78695,-84.72989],thn='North Huron Avenue lot beside the light',brick='no',
  hours='Seasonal, early May to early October: 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., and to 5:30 p.m. in summer; last entry an hour before closing (2024 and 2025 seasons)'),
N('big-sable-point-light-ludington','Big Sable Point Light','Ludington','mi','light','standing',44.05778,-86.51444,'exact','open','hike','','1867–',
  'Congress paid for this light in 1866 and building began in 1867, making it the first light station on this stretch of the shore. The 112-foot tower was first made of cream-coloured brick. The brick wore badly, and in 1900 the whole tower was wrapped in steel plate, which is what you see today, painted white and black. It was the last Great Lakes light to get electricity and plumbing, and it still works as a navigation light. The original lens went to a museum in 1985.',
  'A black-and-white banded steel-clad tower, with the keeper\'s buildings round it that a lightkeepers\' group has restored. Visitors may climb the tower. The brick under the steel cannot be seen.',
  'A steel-wrapped tower at the end of a dune trail.',
  'Inside Ludington State Park on Lake Michigan; the Sable Points Lightkeepers Association looks after the site. A Michigan state park vehicle permit is needed to enter the park. Climbing costs a donation: $8 for adults and $5 for children 17 and under, and children must be 40 inches tall (2026 figures from a web search, not the association\'s page, which could not be opened). On chosen summer days a bus runs from the ranger house inside the park for a fee. Hours are the 2026 season as a web search quoted it. How long the walk is was not checked.',
  [W('Big Sable Point Light'),['Pure Ludington: Big Sable lighthouse season','https://thinkdunes.com/event/big-sable-lighthouse-season/'],['Michigan DNR: Ludington State Park visitor center','https://www.michigan.gov/dnr/places/v-centers/ludington']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',
  hours='2026 season: May 4 to Sept 7, Monday 1 to 5 p.m. and Tuesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sept 10 to Oct 25, Thursday to Sunday from 10 a.m.'),
N('sturgeon-point-light-harrisville','Sturgeon Point Light','Harrisville','mi','light','standing',44.71269,-83.27272,'exact','open','walk','','1869–',
  'The U.S. Lighthouse Board built this station in 1869 to warn ships off a reef that runs 1.5 miles out into Lake Huron. It is a Cape Cod style Great Lakes station: a 70-foot brick tower on an ashlar limestone foundation with a two-story keeper\'s house of the same materials. The first keeper also became the first captain of the surf team that stood watch from here. A brick outhouse of 1869 still stands. In 1982 the Alcona Historical Society leased the light and began a three-year restoration; it now runs a museum in the buildings.',
  'The tower, the keeper\'s quarters museum, the old Bailey School and a gift shop, with the grounds open all year. Museum admission is free, and the tower opens when volunteers are there. The colour of the brick was not checked.',
  'A Cape Cod keeper\'s house at the end of a reef.',
  'Sturgeon Point State Park (Michigan DNR), Haynes Township, Alcona County, with a state park lot a short walk to the south. Museums are run by the Alcona Historical Society. Hours are from the DNR park page and the society\'s 2024 page as a web search quoted them; the 2026 schedule was not read.',
  [W('Sturgeon Point Light'),['Alcona Historical Society: admission','https://alconahistoricalsociety.com/index.php/admission'],['Michigan DNR: Sturgeon Point','https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/sturgeonpoint']],
  conf='mixed',th=[44.71136,-83.27275],thn='Sturgeon Point State Park parking area',brick='?',
  hours='Friday to Sunday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m., Memorial Day to mid-September; tower when volunteers are there (2024 schedule); grounds open all year'),
N('au-sable-light-station-grand-marais','Au Sable Light Station','Grand Marais','mi','light','standing',46.67271,-86.13938,'site','open','hike','','1874–',
  'Built in 1874 on Au Sable Point to guard a sandstone reef that reaches almost a mile into Lake Superior and caught many ships, among them the passenger steamer Lady Elgin, which grounded there in 1859. Colonel Orlando Poe designed the white brick conical tower and its attached keepers\' quarters. A red brick keeper\'s house stands beside it, with a second keepers\' house of 1909, a fog signal building of 1897, a kerosene shed of 1895 and a boathouse of 1875. The National Park Service keeps the station as it looked in 1909 and 1910, when it had two keepers, and the original third-order lens went back into the tower in 1996.',
  'The light tower and a cluster of old brick service buildings, reached on foot along the shore, with shipwreck remains on the beach nearby. In summer a ranger leads a grounds tour and a tower climb. The tower is white brick and the house beside it is red brick.',
  'A lighthouse on a shipwreck coast, a mile and a half from the road.',
  'Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore. Park at the Hurricane River day use area and walk about 1.5 miles each way (the Park Service\'s figure as a web search quoted it). The lighthouse climbs and tours run June 7 to October 15, 2026; the daily times were not found, and a booking listing said tours are first come, first served. A pit toilet and water are at the station.',
  [W('Au Sable Light'),['NPS: Visit the Au Sable Light Station','https://www.nps.gov/piro/planyourvisit/au-sable.htm']],
  conf='mixed',brick='some',
  hours='Climbs and ranger tours June 7 to October 15, 2026; daily times not found; 1.5-mile walk each way from Hurricane River'),
N('old-presque-isle-light-presque-isle','Old Presque Isle Light','Presque Isle','mi','light','standing',45.34204,-83.47827,'exact','open','walk','','1840–1871',
  'The first light at Presque Isle, on Lake Huron, was built in 1840 with Congress\'s money, of stone and brick. The tower and house weathered badly, and by 1867 Congress found them worn enough to pay for a new station nearby. The old light is no longer in use and is a museum. Lake stories say a former caretaker\'s ghost still lights the lamp.',
  'A short stone tower, about thirty feet high according to the township\'s listing, with the keeper\'s cottage beside it as a hands-on museum. A newer, much taller light stands nearby and is a separate site. Whether the 1840 fabric can be seen inside was not checked.',
  'The first light on this part of Lake Huron, outlived by a taller one beside it.',
  'Run by Presque Isle Township and its museum society. The 2025 season ran Friday to Monday, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., closed Tuesday to Thursday, with tower tickets sold until 4:15 p.m. (township event page as a web search quoted it; 2026 not read). Admission prices are inconsistent between listings. The mapped lot found is 620 m west, on East Grand Lake Road, and not on public land, so ask at the museum where to park.',
  [W('Old Presque Isle Light'),['Presque Isle Township: lighthouses open for the season','https://presqueisletwp.org/event/lighthouses-open-for-the-season/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',
  hours='Friday to Monday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., late May to autumn (2025 season); closed Tuesday to Thursday'),
N('pointe-aux-barques-light-port-hope','Pointe aux Barques Light','Port Hope','mi','light','standing',44.02284,-82.79438,'site','open','walk','','1848–',
  'The first light here, a 65-foot stone tower with a keeper\'s house, was lit in 1848. Its first keeper, Peter Shook, drowned on a supply trip within the year, and his widow Catherine took over as Michigan\'s first woman lighthouse keeper. The old buildings had decayed by the 1850s, so the present tower, joined to the keeper\'s house and built of Milwaukee Cream City brick, went up in 1857. The tower and dwelling, an assistant keeper\'s house of 1908, an oil house of 1892 and an outhouse of 1908 all remain. A life-saving station of 15 buildings stood here until 1913.',
  'The keeper\'s house is a museum of the light and its keepers, with the original third-order lens on show. The pale brick is buff, not red.',
  'Michigan\'s first woman keeper took over this light after her husband drowned.',
  'Lighthouse Park, Huron County, at the tip of the Thumb. The museum\'s own page gives Wednesday to Sunday, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., to a closing date of September 29 with no year, and another page says Memorial Day weekend to September; the tower and the life-saving station open only on special days. Admission was $5 for adults in an older chamber listing. Park in Lighthouse Park off Gulick Drive.',
  [W('Pointe aux Barques Light'),['Pointe aux Barques Maritime Museum','https://pointeauxbarqueslighthouse.org']],
  conf='mixed',th=[44.02307,-82.79665],thn='Lighthouse Park lot off Gulick Drive',brick='no',
  hours='Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Memorial Day weekend to late September (year not stated on the page); tower on special days'),
N('glen-haven-village-sleeping-bear','Glen Haven village','Glen Arbor','mi','town','standing',44.89889,-86.03028,'area','open','walk','','1857–1931',
  'Glen Haven began in 1857 as Sleeping Bearville, when C. C. McCarty built a sawmill and inn on the beach, and grew into a Lake Michigan port that sold firewood to steamers. David H. Day bought the village in 1881. A warehouse on the shore was later a cherry cannery in the 1920s and is now the Cannery Boathouse Museum, which holds small wooden boats that crossed the Manitou Passage. The Coast Guard life-saving station of 1901, moved to its present spot in 1931, is now a maritime museum.',
  'A restored general store and a working blacksmith shop, the boathouse museum and the life-saving station, all on the shore of Sleeping Bear Bay. The National Park Service says the buildings are staffed in summer.',
  'A lake port village kept as a museum inside a national lakeshore.',
  'Sleeping Bear Dunes National Lakeshore. The Park Service says the village buildings are open and staffed from Memorial Day to Labor Day; daily times were not found, and the general store is closed outside summer. Restrooms are by the general store. The pin is the village in general: it is the Wikipedia point.',
  [W('Glen Haven, Michigan'),['NPS: Glen Haven Historic Village','https://www.nps.gov/slbe/planyourvisit/glen-haven.htm'],['NPS: Glen Haven General Store','https://www.nps.gov/places/000/glen-haven-general-store.htm']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',
  hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day, staffed by volunteers; daily times not found'),
N('port-oneida-rural-historic-district','Port Oneida Rural Historic District','Glen Arbor','mi','town','standing',44.93722,-85.93639,'area','public','drive','','1852–1940s',
  'Carsten and Elizabeth Burfiend arrived in 1852, and by 1860 eighty-seven people lived at the port. Logging gave way to farming in the 1890s when the pines were gone, and the dock and village were abandoned by 1908. Families kept farming until the Second World War. After Sleeping Bear Dunes National Lakeshore was set up in 1970 the National Park Service took over most of the land and buildings. The district covers about 3,400 acres along Lake Michigan and went on the National Register in 2017.',
  'Farmhouses, barns and outbuildings along M-22 and nearby roads, kept in repair by the Park Service. The Charles and Hattie Olsen Farm, about two miles north of Glen Arbor on M-22, holds the Port Oneida Heritage Center. Most buildings are looked at from the road.',
  'Lake farms of the 1800s left standing in a national park.',
  'National Park Service land in Sleeping Bear Dunes National Lakeshore. Park at the lots at the Bay View Trailhead or the farm pull-offs; no opening hours were found for the Olsen farm, and the buildings are open mainly during the August Port Oneida Fair. The pin is a point in the district, not one building. A mapped lot at Dechow Farm is on private land.',
  [W('Port Oneida Rural Historic District'),['NPS: Port Oneida','https://www.nps.gov/slbe/planyourvisit/portoneida.htm']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',th=[44.93416,-85.94946],thn='Bay View Trailhead, Sleeping Bear Heritage Trail'),
N('packard-automotive-plant-detroit','Packard Automotive Plant','Detroit','mi','industry','ruins',42.37962,-83.02893,'area','view','walk','','1903–1956',
  'Albert Kahn Associates designed this vast car works on East Grand Boulevard for the Packard Motor Car Company. It opened in 1903 and made Packard cars until 1956; Wikipedia counts it as the first factory in the United States to use reinforced concrete for car-making. At its peak it employed 40,000 people. The city took most of the land in 1994 for unpaid taxes. Demolition began on Building 21 in October 2022 and on Building 28 in January 2023, and the city then stopped, so the site stands empty and partly torn down.',
  'Long concrete factory blocks, stripped and dark, along East Grand Boulevard, with the Grand Boulevard bridge, which has collapsed. Building 22, the newer Merlin-engine addition, is still in use by a firm that makes props and displays.',
  'The biggest car factory ruin in Detroit, half torn down.',
  'Private and city-owned land, unsafe and fenced; look from the public street only. An organised public tour was held on August 12, 2017; whether others run now was not checked. Do not enter the buildings. The pin is the Wikipedia point; the factory covers 40 acres.',
  [W('Packard Automotive Plant')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('ramsdell-theatre-manistee','Ramsdell Theatre','Manistee','mi','civic','standing',44.24575,-86.32353,'exact','open','walk','','1902–',
  'Thomas Jefferson Ramsdell, the town\'s only lawyer, paid for this opera house after fire destroyed two earlier ones, in 1882 and 1900. Solon Spencer Beman designed it, and it opened on September 4, 1903, at a cost of $100,000. It was largely unused for plays from the 1920s until 1951, when women\'s clubs set out to restore it. James Earl Jones began his acting career on this stage. It is a National Historic Landmark and a Michigan Historic Site.',
  'A red brick front with a colonnaded porch and ornamental brick panels, and inside a dome painted with Venus in a chariot ringed by cherubs. The seating has been cut from 1,200 to 489, and the upper balcony is closed to the public.',
  'An opera house a lawyer built after two burned.',
  'City-centre street parking and lots on Water and Poplar Streets. The business office takes ticket orders at the First Street entrance, Monday, Tuesday, Thursday and Friday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., and says the theatre is closed on Wednesday, Saturday and Sunday unless there is an event (the theatre\'s ticket policy page as a web search quoted it). Tours exist but no schedule was found; ask the business office on 231 398-9770.',
  [W('Ramsdell Theatre'),['Ramsdell Theatre: ticket policy','https://ramsdelltheatre.org/house-policies/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='some',
  hours='Business office Monday, Tuesday, Thursday and Friday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; open for shows and events; tours by arrangement'),
N('ypsilanti-water-tower','Ypsilanti Water Tower','Ypsilanti','mi','water','standing',42.24556,-83.62469,'exact','view','walk','','1889–1890',
  'Built in 1890 on the highest ground in Ypsilanti as part of a city waterworks begun in 1889, at a cost of about $21,400. William R. Coats designed it in a Queen Anne style, of Joliet limestone, 147 feet tall with a base 85 feet across, and it was the town\'s only water tower until 1956. In that year the American Water Works Association named it a landmark. The builders cut at least four crosses into the stonework, one above the west door and a hard-to-find Greek cross on the east side. A marble bust of Demetrios Ypsilantis stands at its foot. Locals nickname it for its shape, and it has a place in student legend.',
  'The limestone tower stands on Summit Street. It is seen from the street and its small park; the inside was not checked, and a source does not say it is open.',
  'A stone water tower with hidden crosses in its walls.',
  'On Summit Street in Normal Park. It is a landmark looked at from the street. Mapped parking nearby is mostly Eastern Michigan University lots with their own rules; use street parking where allowed.',
  [W('Ypsilanti Water Tower')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
